\meta{status}{implemented}
\meta{created}{2026-11-24}
\meta{updated}{2026-11-25}
\meta{origin}{docs/PLATFORM.md}
\import{base-macros}

\p{\strong{Status:} implemented, and revised when the release became one executable: \src{scripts/build.sh} builds the native image alone by default, and builds the JVM launcher beside it only into a development distribution; see [packaging](design-packaging). The JVM line below was wired in on 2026-11-24 (\commit{ca8e886}), and the arena definition builds and runs the image from a clean copy.}

\p{\strong{Two launchers, one set of jars.} A development build (\code{\verb~|scripts/build.sh --dev~}) makes a distribution with the jars of every module, \code{\verb~|bin/rlvm~} and \code{\verb~|bin/rlvm-jvm~}; the release is \code{\verb~|bin/rlvm~} alone, the single executable a user runs. \code{\verb~|bin/rlvm~} is a native image of GraalVM CE 25 built from exactly those jars, with Truffle runtime compilation linked in. \code{\verb~|bin/rlvm-jvm~} is a shell script that runs the same jars on the HotSpot VM of the same GraalVM, with C2 as the JIT. Which launcher is for what was [a decision of its own](dec-WX9Z): the JVM launcher the warm platform, the native image the platform of a cold process and of the arena. Since the release is one executable the JVM launcher is a tool of the repository, for the warm measurements of the jars, the gates and the test kit, and is never packaged. The same executable built with Oracle GraalVM and profile-guided optimisation exists only on request, as an option of the build, see [dec-6E82](dec-6E82) and [packaging](design-packaging).}

\p{\strong{The launcher proper} is the module \code{rlvm-cli}. It parses the command line, finds a checker through \code{ServiceLoader} (the kernel registers from \code{rlvm-main}, which holds no kernel logic and finds the engines the same way), runs it on a thread of its own and is the only place that ends the process. The exit status is fail closed: it starts as 3 and receives a verdict's status in one place, only if the checker returned normally, the verdict is not null and no thread died.}

\table{
\tr{\th{Status}\th{Meaning}}
\tr{\td{0}\td{accept: only an accept returned cleanly by the checker}}
\tr{\td{1}\td{reject}}
\tr{\td{2}\td{decline}}
\tr{\td{3}\td{internal error: any throwable, a null verdict, a thread that died, heap exhaustion}}
\tr{\td{64}\td{usage error: a bad option or setting, a missing export; raised before the export is opened}}
}

\p{A JVM or an image that fails before \code{main} exits with 1, which would read as a rejection. The arena run line therefore asks the launcher to add 10 to every status it produces itself and maps exactly 10, 11 and 12 back (\code{DIST} stands for the directory of the built distribution):}

\pre{\verb~|
RLVM_STATUS_BASE=10 DIST/bin/rlvm --threads 8 "$IN"; case $? in 10) exit 0;; 11) exit 1;; 12) exit 2;; *) exit 3;; esac
~}

\p{\strong{Settings.} Kernel settings are \code{\verb~|-X KEY=VALUE~}: among them \code{\verb~|policy=practical|strict~} (see [the exactness contract](design-exactness-contract)), \code{\verb~|engine=NAME~}, \code{\verb~|load=batch|pipelined|auto~}, \code{\verb~|passes=N~} for warm passes (see [the measurement method](design-measurement-method)), and every key an engine or [the driver](design-driver) declares. An unknown key is status 64, never ignored. Since 2026-11-24 a run whose command line says nothing is the target configuration of the metric: the engine is the machine with its default switch set, [since the evening of 2026-11-24](log-65Z0), and a run that means the reference engine names it; the load is pipelined when two or more workers run and the input is a file of at least 8 MiB; one scanner thread reads files of 256 MiB and more (two until that evening, see [the settings of a plain run](dec-EGMG)); the worker count is one per CPU, at most 8. The arena run line names 8 workers; it was 4, the count by CPU time.}

\p{\strong{The JVM line.} The script passes these flags, then a heap chosen by engine:}

\pre{\verb~|
-XX:+UseSerialGC -XX:-UseJVMCICompiler -XX:CICompilerCount=8 -XX:+UseTransparentHugePages
-Xlog:disable -Xlog:all=warning:stderr -XX:+ExitOnOutOfMemoryError
--enable-native-access=ALL-UNNAMED --sun-misc-unsafe-memory-access=allow
heap, machine:           -Xmx7g -Xmn1g           (no -Xms)
heap, any other engine:  -Xms13g -Xmx13g -Xmn1g
~}

\p{The engine of a run is what \code{\verb~|-X engine=NAME~} says, else the default engine of the distribution, which the build asks the jars for and writes into the script; the strict policy means the reference engine. A heap flag on the command line replaces the default heap. The class path is spelled out jar by jar in sorted order, because the VM expands a wildcard in directory order and with it the order of the service providers. Why each flag is there, and why there is no AOT cache, is in [dec-E3EX](dec-E3EX); why the collector is Serial is in [dec-Z6DD](dec-Z6DD).}

\p{\strong{The native image} is built with \code{\verb~|-O3 --gc=serial --no-fallback -R:+ExitOnOutOfMemoryError -R:MaxHeapSize=13g -R:InitialCollectionPolicy=BySpaceAndTime~}; the collection policy was [chosen by measurement](dec-EGMG), and \code{\verb~|rlvm --version~} prints the settings that are baked in. \code{\verb~|-O3~} needs 10 to 18 \% less CPU time than \code{\verb~|-O2~} for the machine. The heap limit is a limit and not a reservation. Since 2026-11-24 the image is also built with the machine's inline lists, rule H10 of [the control convention](design-control-convention-h): force-inline of every continuation step of the machine and never-inline of its slow paths, generated at build time from the machine jar; if that fails the image is built without them. On matched images they took 0.7 to 4.4 \% off the machine's CPU time of a warm pass on Init, Std, the Cedar slice and cedar, with reachability inside its spread and the wall clock equal (8 workers). As of 2026-11-24 [design-switches-document](design-switches-document) still describes the lists as measured and not adopted; the build script is the newer state.}

\p{\strong{The hand-kept inline list.} Since 2026-11-25 the force-inline list of the image has a second source, \src{rlvm-machine/h10/force-inline.txt}: 75 hot methods named by hand, which \src{rlvm-machine/h10/native-image-args.sh} adds to the generated ones, so that the build record of a distribution reads 269 forced and 81 never where it read 194 and 81. Without a profile the Community Edition inliner cannot tell a hot call from a cold one; the list names the readers of one line (\code{\verb~|Terms.a~}, \code{\verb~|Terms.b~}, \code{\verb~|Runs.get~}) and the steps with one or two call sites that a profile of the executable showed as calls, eight of them methods of \code{rlvm-store}. It changes no class file, so the jars and the JVM launcher are untouched; in the image it removes 9.6 \% of the instructions of an Init pass with one worker and 4 to 6 \% of the CPU time of a warm pass with 8 workers, and the geometric mean of the eight warm passes is 0.966 of the base's (three interleaved rounds, no corpus slower). An entry is a name: an entry of the machine that names no method is reported by the script, but if a listed method of \code{rlvm-store} is renamed or split, the entry stops matching and the build does not notice. A new hot helper gets an entry, not a split into a fast and a slow method: three such splits were measured and not adopted, because C2 does not inline the methods they move out of line and reachability got 3.6 \% slower on the JVM launcher. See [the changelog entry](log-E7BA), [the report](rep-OPT-A) and [the decision](dec-JT59).}

\p{\strong{Verification and stacks.} Every artifact is verified before the distribution is replaced as a whole: the exit-status self-test in every mode, a real heap exhaustion under a 128 MB cap that must end with status 3, and a probe that fails unless a Truffle call target reaches last-tier compiled code inside the artifact. Worker threads and the environment thread get a deep stack, a reservation whose pages are committed only when touched, because the reference engine recurses like the original kernel and runs on those threads.}

\p{\strong{What was measured.} The same jars on both launchers, machine engine with the switch set \code{\verb~|warm,gated,expect~}, 8 workers, load pipelined, every timing inside a quiet-core window on the aarch64 development host; warm is the median of the later passes of one process, cold a fresh process. The JVM rows use the line above.}

\table{
\tr{\th{Corpus}\th{Warm wall, JVM (s)}\th{Warm wall, native image (s)}\th{Cold wall, JVM (s)}\th{Cold wall, native image (s)}}
\tr{\td{Init}\td{0.81}\td{1.02}\td{3.57}\td{1.18}}
\tr{\td{Std}\td{1.37}\td{1.72}\td{3.11}\td{1.78}}
\tr{\td{cedar}\td{1.92}\td{2.54}\td{5.15}\td{2.5}}
\tr{\td{reachability}\td{1.65}\td{2.44}\td{2.54}\td{2.63}}
\tr{\td{Mathlib, pass 2 and pass 1 of one process}\td{24.91}\td{28.34}\td{27.16}\td{29.10}}
}

\p{Warm, the JVM launcher needs 0.68 to 0.88 times the image's wall clock; cold, up to three times as long below Mathlib, at 1.5 to 2.4 times the image's resident set. On the prelude alone (3.8 MB) the image answers in 0.02 s and the JVM in about 0.2 s. Not measured: x86-64, the architecture of the arena runner; a host whose transparent huge pages are set to \code{never}.}

